  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 335 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nModel guidance is fairly consistent that moisture levels will remain \nelevated and above climatological norms with PWATs staying above 2\" \nacross South Florida going into the upcoming holiday weekend and \ninto early next week. The subtropical surface ridge axis located \njust offshore of the Mid-Atlantic United States will continue to be \ndisplaced to the southeast into the open southwest Atlantic waters \nas we head into Friday and the weekend. Meanwhile in the upper \nlevels, an expansive ridge that had been dominating the eastern \nsection of the United States and keeping any and all significant \nweather features well to our north will finally start to erode. \nLooking further north, a large upper trough located over Atlantic \nCanada/New England will swing south and eastward during the latter \npart of the weekend and into early next week. An associated surface \nlow pressure system and cold front will respond accordingly by \ndropping into the southeastern United States and becoming quasi-\nstationary thereafter over the Florida peninsula. At the same time, \nassociated shortwave troughing will also look to drop southward in \ntandem with our surface cold front on the southwestern flank of the \naforementioned upper trough, though the orientation of this vort max \nand the most robust dynamics will remain to the north of South \nFlorida.\n\nALl in all, the presence of enhanced moisture will keep scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms in the forecast through Labor Day \nweekend and into the early part of next week. Our subtropical ridge, \nas it undergoes a slight westward expansion, will help induce a more \nestablished southeasterly flow through the first half of the \nweekend, thus allowing daytime convection to primarily favor the \ninterior portions of South Florida as the Gulf sea breeze also pushes \ninland each afternoon. We will look to lose the influence of our \nsubtropical ridge during the second half of the weekend and into the \nLabor Day holiday as we see it retreat into the southwest Atlantic \nand be gradually replaced by a southward-advancing cold front. As a \nresult, we will notice a wind shift from southeasterly to more \nsouthwesterly across much of the peninsula, leading to favored \nshower and thunderstorm chances across our eastern metros. As with \nany pattern that has convection pinned to the east coast, localized \nflooding will be a legitimate concern in low-lying/poor drainage \nareas. The overall severe threat will be low throughout this long-\nterm period as we expect mid-level temperatures to remain warmer \nthan average (falling within the 50th and 90th percentiles), shear \nto stay relatively light, and no significant dry air intrusions to \npromote microburst potential. The cold front, which will stay draped \nnorth of our region, will slowly dissipate after Labor Day through \nmid-week, allowing high pressure to build behind it and thus \nreintroducing an easterly flow regime to the peninsula. In addition \nto the heavy rainfall threat, cloud-to-ground lightning will perhaps \nbe our most pressing convective hazard this weekend with many of us \nlikely spending extended periods of time outdoors in our \nfestivities. Always remember, when thunder roars, go indoors. Remain \nsheltered for at least 30 min after you have heard the last clap of \nthunder. Highs are forecast to remain in the low to mid 90s with \nmaximum apparent temperatures reaching the 100-105 degree range each \nafternoon preceding the initiation of daily convection. \nWidespread moderate to locally major HeatRisk is expected to \ngovern both of our coastal metros.",
